            0.119
          ],
          "content": "122"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.138,
            0.398,
            0.157
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Trigonometric Ratios of $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.164,
            0.594,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "In $\\Delta   \\mathrm { A B C } ,$ right-angled at B, if one angle is $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , then the other angle is also $4 5 ^ { \\circ } , \\mathrm { i . e . } , \\angle \\mathrm { A } = \\angle \\mathrm { C } = 4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ (see Fig. 8.14)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.228,
            0.135,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.292,
            0.227,
            0.51,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{BC} = \\mathrm{AB} \\quad (\\mathrm{Why?})",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.713,
            0.137,
            0.893,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.253,
            0.384,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "Now, Suppose $\\mathrm{BC} = \\mathrm{AB} = a.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.759,
            0.263,
            0.858,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 8.14"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.278,
            0.726,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "Then by Pythagoras Theorem, $AC^{2} = AB^{2} + BC^{2} = a^{2} + a^{2} = 2a^{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.309,
            0.417,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "and, therefore, $\\mathrm { A C } =   a { \\sqrt { 2 } }   .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.341,
            0.653,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": "Using the definitions of the trigonometric ratios, we have :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.373,
            0.736,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "\\sin 45^{\\circ} = \\frac{ side opposite to angle   45^{\\circ}}{ hyponeuse } = \\frac{BC}{AC} = \\frac{a}{a\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.435,
            0.735,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "\\cos 45^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\overline{ side adjacent to angle 45^{\\circ}}}{\\overline{ hypotenuse }} = \\frac{\\overline{AB}}{\\overline{AC}} = \\frac{a}{a\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.498,
            0.682,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "\\tan 45^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\overline{side opposite to angle 45^{\\circ}}}{\\overline{side adjacent to angle 45^{\\circ}}} = \\frac{\\overline{BC}}{\\overline{AB}} = \\frac{a}{a} = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.563,
            0.896,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "Also, cosec $45^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\sin 45^{\\circ}} = \\sqrt{2}$ , sec $45^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 45^{\\circ}} = \\sqrt{2}$ , cot $4 5 ^ { \\circ } = { \\frac { 1 } { \\tan   4 5 ^ { \\circ } } }   =   1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.624,
            0.485,
            0.644
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Trigonometric Ratios of $\\mathbf { 3 0 ^ { \\circ } }$ and $\\mathbf { 6 0 ^ { \\circ } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.65,
            0.595,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": "Let us now calculate the trigonometric ratios of $3 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . Consider an equilateral triangle ABC. Since each angle in an equilateral triangle is $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , therefore, $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{C} = 60^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.733,
            0.593,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": "Draw the perpendicular AD from A to the side BC (see Fig. 8.15)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.667,
            0.641,
            0.862,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.716,
                0.769,
                0.815,
                0.787
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 8.15"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.776,
            0.153,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": "Now"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.389,
            0.775,
            0.662,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "\\Delta \\: \\mathrm { A B D } \\cong \\Delta \\: \\mathrm { A C D } \\quad \\mathrm { ( W h y ? ) }",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.798,
            0.204,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.428,
            0.797,
            0.535,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { B D } = \\mathrm { D C }",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.821,
            0.142,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": "and"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.819,
            0.667,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "\\angle  BAD = \\angle  CAD  \\left(  CPCT  \\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_26.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.843,
            0.281,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "Now observe that:"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.864,
            0.901,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": "ABD is a right triangle, right - angled at D with $\\angle BAD = 30^{\\circ}   and   \\angle ABD = 60^{\\circ}$ (see Fig. 8.15)."
        }
      ]
    }
